Separate the two vertical right-angled triangles. Their shared side is the mast height $OT$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,0)--(3,0)--(3,2)--cycle;
\draw (2.75,0)--(2.75,0.25)--(3,0.25);
\path (0,0) node[below] {$A$} (3,0) node[below] {$O$} (3,2) node[above] {$T$};
\path (1.3,1.15) node[above left] {$26\text{ m}$} (0.7,0.2) node[above] {$30^{\circ}$};
\draw (5,0)--(8,0)--(8,2)--cycle;
\draw (7.75,0)--(7.75,0.25)--(8,0.25);
\path (5,0) node[below] {$B$} (8,0) node[below] {$O$} (8,2) node[above] {$T$};
\path (6.5,0) node[below] {$20\text{ m}$} (8,1) node[right] {$OT$} (5.7,0.2) node[above] {$\theta$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

In $\triangle AOT$:
$\begin{align*}\sin30^{\circ}&=\frac{OT}{26}\\OT&=26\sin30^{\circ}\\OT&=13\text{ m}\end{align*}$

In $\triangle BOT$:
$\begin{align*}\tan\theta&=\frac{13}{20}\\\theta&=\tan^{-1}\left(\frac{13}{20}\right)\\\theta&\approx33.0^{\circ}\end{align*}$

The angles of elevation from the boats equal the corresponding angles of depression. The bearings give $\angle POQ=120^{\circ}-30^{\circ}=90^{\circ}$.

In the vertical triangles:
$\begin{align*}OP&=\frac{40}{\tan45^{\circ}}\\OP&=40\text{ m}\end{align*}$
$\begin{align*}OQ&=\frac{40}{\tan30^{\circ}}\\OQ&=40\sqrt{3}\text{ m}\end{align*}$

Use these exact lengths in the horizontal triangle.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,2.5)--(0,0)--(4,0)--cycle;
\draw (0,0.25)--(0.25,0.25)--(0.25,0);
\path (0,2.5) node[above] {$P$} (0,0) node[below left] {$O$} (4,0) node[below right] {$Q$};
\path (0,1.25) node[left] {$40\text{ m}$} (2,0) node[below] {$40\sqrt{3}\text{ m}$} (2,1.4) node[above right] {$PQ$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$\begin{align*}PQ^{2}&=OP^{2}+OQ^{2}\\PQ^{2}&=40^{2}+(40\sqrt{3})^{2}\\PQ^{2}&=6400\\PQ&=80\text{ m}\end{align*}$

The projection of $AG$ onto the base is $AC$, so the required angle is $\angle CAG$. First find the base diagonal.

$\begin{align*}AC^{2}&=9^{2}+12^{2}\\AC^{2}&=225\\AC&=15\text{ cm}\end{align*}$

Since $CG$ is perpendicular to the base, $\triangle ACG$ is right-angled at $C$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,0)--(4,0)--(4,2)--cycle;
\draw (3.75,0)--(3.75,0.25)--(4,0.25);
\path (0,0) node[below left] {$A$} (4,0) node[below right] {$C$} (4,2) node[above] {$G$};
\path (2,0) node[below] {$15\text{ cm}$} (4,1) node[right] {$7\text{ cm}$} (0.8,0.15) node[above] {$\theta$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$\begin{align*}\tan\theta&=\frac{CG}{AC}\\\tan\theta&=\frac{7}{15}\\\theta&=\tan^{-1}\left(\frac{7}{15}\right)\\\theta&\approx25^{\circ}\end{align*}$

Both $OM$ and $TM$ are perpendicular to $AB$, so the angle between the planes is $\angle TMO$.

$OM=\frac{20}{2}=10\text{ cm}$.

The perpendicular cross-section is $\triangle TOM$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,0)--(3,0)--(3,3)--cycle;
\draw (2.75,0)--(2.75,0.25)--(3,0.25);
\path (0,0) node[below left] {$M$} (3,0) node[below right] {$O$} (3,3) node[above] {$T$};
\path (1.5,0) node[below] {$10\text{ cm}$} (3,1.5) node[right] {$14\text{ cm}$} (0.6,0.3) node[above] {$\theta$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$\begin{align*}\tan\theta&=\frac{OT}{OM}\\\tan\theta&=\frac{14}{10}\\\theta&=\tan^{-1}\left(\frac{14}{10}\right)\\\theta&\approx54.5^{\circ}\end{align*}$

The projection of $VA$ onto the base is $OA$, so the required angle is $\angle VAO$. Find half the base diagonal.

$\begin{align*}AC^{2}&=12^{2}+12^{2}\\AC&=12\sqrt{2}\text{ cm}\\OA&=\frac{AC}{2}\\OA&=6\sqrt{2}\text{ cm}\end{align*}$

Use $\triangle AOV$, right-angled at $O$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,0)--(3,0)--(3,3)--cycle;
\draw (2.75,0)--(2.75,0.25)--(3,0.25);
\path (0,0) node[below left] {$A$} (3,0) node[below right] {$O$} (3,3) node[above] {$V$};
\path (1.5,0) node[below] {$6\sqrt{2}\text{ cm}$} (1.5,1.5) node[above left] {$12\text{ cm}$} (0.6,0.25) node[above] {$\alpha$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$\begin{align*}\cos\alpha&=\frac{OA}{VA}\\\cos\alpha&=\frac{6\sqrt{2}}{12}\\\cos\alpha&=\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\\\alpha&=45^{\circ}\end{align*}$

Express both ground distances using the same unknown height.

$\begin{align*}\tan30^{\circ}&=\frac{h}{FP}\\FP&=\frac{h}{\tan30^{\circ}}\\FP&=\sqrt{3}h\end{align*}$
$\begin{align*}\tan45^{\circ}&=\frac{h}{FQ}\\FQ&=\frac{h}{\tan45^{\circ}}\\FQ&=h\end{align*}$

South and east are perpendicular, so $\triangle PFQ$ is right-angled at $F$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,3)--(0,0)--(2,0)--cycle;
\draw (0,0.25)--(0.25,0.25)--(0.25,0);
\path (0,3) node[above] {$P$} (0,0) node[below left] {$F$} (2,0) node[below right] {$Q$};
\path (0,1.5) node[left] {$\sqrt{3}h$} (1,0) node[below] {$h$} (1,1.5) node[above right] {$100\text{ m}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$\begin{align*}FP^{2}+FQ^{2}&=PQ^{2}\\(\sqrt{3}h)^{2}+h^{2}&=100^{2}\\4h^{2}&=10000\\h^{2}&=2500\\h&=50\text{ m}\end{align*}$

Take the positive root because $h$ is a height.

The ground triangle is right-angled at $A$: $AF$ points north and $AB$ points east.

From the vertical triangles:
$\begin{align*}AF&=\frac{h}{\tan45^{\circ}}\\AF&=h\end{align*}$
$\begin{align*}BF&=\frac{h}{\tan30^{\circ}}\\BF&=\sqrt{3}h\end{align*}$

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,2.5)--(0,0)--(3,0)--cycle;
\draw (0,0.25)--(0.25,0.25)--(0.25,0);
\path (0,2.5) node[above] {$F$} (0,0) node[below left] {$A$} (3,0) node[below right] {$B$};
\path (0,1.25) node[left] {$h$} (1.5,0) node[below] {$60\text{ m}$} (1.5,1.25) node[above right] {$\sqrt{3}h$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$\begin{align*}BF^{2}&=AF^{2}+AB^{2}\\(\sqrt{3}h)^{2}&=h^{2}+60^{2}\\2h^{2}&=3600\\h^{2}&=1800\\h&=30\sqrt{2}\text{ m}\\h&\approx42.4\text{ m}\end{align*}$

Since $AO$ points north, the bearing gives $\angle OAB=60^{\circ}$. The horizontal triangle is right-angled at $O$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,0)--(0,2)--(3,2)--cycle;
\draw (0,1.75)--(0.25,1.75)--(0.25,2);
\path (0,0) node[below] {$A$} (0,2) node[above left] {$O$} (3,2) node[above right] {$B$};
\path (0,1) node[left] {$AO$} (1.6,0.9) node[below right] {$80\text{ m}$} (0.35,0.7) node[right] {$60^{\circ}$};
\draw (5,0)--(8,0)--(8,1.8)--cycle;
\draw (7.75,0)--(7.75,0.25)--(8,0.25);
\path (5,0) node[below] {$A$} (8,0) node[below] {$O$} (8,1.8) node[above] {$T$};
\path (6.5,0) node[below] {$AO$} (8,0.9) node[right] {$h$} (5.6,0.15) node[above] {$30^{\circ}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

In the horizontal triangle:
$\begin{align*}\cos60^{\circ}&=\frac{AO}{80}\\AO&=80\cos60^{\circ}\\AO&=40\text{ m}\end{align*}$

In the vertical triangle:
$\begin{align*}\tan30^{\circ}&=\frac{h}{40}\\h&=40\tan30^{\circ}\\h&=\frac{40}{\sqrt{3}}\text{ m}\\h&\approx23.1\text{ m}\end{align*}$
